1: // Per-model output-token ceilings learned from provider rejections.
2: //
3: // Clients size max_tokens for the model they think they are talking to:
4: // Claude Code asks for 128000, Open WebUI for 65536. Several free models stop
5: // lower and reject the request outright instead of clamping it, observed live:
6: //   - Ollama Cloud: "max_tokens (128000) exceeds model's maximum output tokens
7: //     (65536) for model nemotron-3-ultra"
8: //   - Groq: "`max_tokens` must be less than or equal to `65536`, the maximum
9: //     value for `max_tokens` is less than the `context_window` for this model"
10: // The catalog has no output-limit column, so the ceiling is learned from the
11: // first rejection and applied to every later request on that model through the
12: // per-route output budget (resolveMaxTokens clamps max_tokens to it). A
13: // restart forgets the ceilings; the first request after it pays one failover
14: // hop to relearn each one.
15: //
16: // Keyed per model AND per endpoint (`modelStatsKey`), not per model: every
17: // custom relay shares platform 'custom', so a ceiling learned from relay A
18: // would silently clamp relay B's replies to relay A's output limit until
19: // restart. Same reasoning as lib/tool-capability.ts (#1230) and the
20: // endpoint_scope identity migration (#651): output ceilings are a property of
21: // the model behind ONE relay, and sibling relays do not share it.
